WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:03.713
In this lesson you'll learn how your computer

2
00:00:03.713 --> 00:00:06.012
decides which program opens a file,

3
00:00:06.012 --> 00:00:08.044
and how you change that decision.

4
00:00:08.044 --> 00:00:10.876
The extension is a label in the file's name,

5
00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:12.328
your computer keeps a table

6
00:00:12.328 --> 00:00:14.835
that matches each label to a program,

7
00:00:14.835 --> 00:00:17.761
renaming changes the label but never the bytes,

8
00:00:17.761 --> 00:00:19.920
and Open with, set to always,

9
00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.639
and default apps are how you edit that table.

10
00:00:23.101 --> 00:00:25.807
AI tools now make files and pick their labels;

11
00:00:25.807 --> 00:00:28.653
the table still decides what opens them.

12
00:00:29.103 --> 00:00:31.321
We're still in District One, The Machine,

13
00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:33.526
which asks what's on your computer.

14
00:00:33.526 --> 00:00:36.278
This is its second stop.

15
00:00:36.278 --> 00:00:40.121
Last lesson, a file became a string of beads,

16
00:00:40.121 --> 00:00:44.033
the bytes, with a paper tag tied on: its name.

17
00:00:44.033 --> 00:00:47.423
Aaron, who made this course, has an IT degree.

18
00:00:47.423 --> 00:00:50.941
He still saved Python in Notepad as .txt,

19
00:00:50.941 --> 00:00:52.450
and didn't know that renaming

20
00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:55.121
it to .py was the fix.

21
00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:56.618
Nobody had told him.

22
00:00:56.618 --> 00:00:59.253
This lesson is the why behind that rename:

23
00:00:59.253 --> 00:01:01.250
one small change to a tag,

24
00:01:01.250 --> 00:01:03.771
and a different program answers.

25
00:01:04.222 --> 00:01:06.080
Now meet the porter.

26
00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:07.566
When you double-click a file,

27
00:01:07.566 --> 00:01:09.446
the porter takes the string.

28
00:01:09.446 --> 00:01:11.780
On Windows that's File Explorer;

29
00:01:11.780 --> 00:01:13.835
on a Mac, Finder.

30
00:01:13.835 --> 00:01:16.122
The porter never looks at the beads.

31
00:01:16.122 --> 00:01:17.643
It reads only the tag,

32
00:01:17.643 --> 00:01:20.824
and only the part after the last dot.

33
00:01:20.824 --> 00:01:22.961
Then it turns to a ledger on the desk,

34
00:01:22.961 --> 00:01:26.699
runs a finger down to the row for .txt,

35
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:30.716
and reads the door written there: Notepad.

36
00:01:30.716 --> 00:01:32.550
It carries the string to that door

37
00:01:32.550 --> 00:01:34.222
and hands it over.

38
00:01:34.222 --> 00:01:37.275
The beads are never untied on the way.

39
00:01:37.275 --> 00:01:39.249
That ledger has a proper name:

40
00:01:39.249 --> 00:01:40.944
file associations.

41
00:01:40.944 --> 00:01:43.359
And there's one ledger per user account,

42
00:01:43.359 --> 00:01:45.576
so two people sharing a PC can get

43
00:01:45.576 --> 00:01:48.643
different programs for the same file.

44
00:01:49.094 --> 00:01:52.426
Look closer at one row, and it's more than a door.

45
00:01:52.426 --> 00:01:55.503
A row holds the program that opens the label,

46
00:01:55.503 --> 00:01:58.730
the icon stamped on every file wearing it,

47
00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:01.482
and the type's name you see in the folder,

48
00:02:01.482 --> 00:02:04.593
like Python file or Text document.

49
00:02:04.593 --> 00:02:06.312
It also holds the commands

50
00:02:06.312 --> 00:02:08.471
on that type's right-click menu.

51
00:02:08.471 --> 00:02:10.770
That's why a renamed file's icon

52
00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:13.301
changes the instant you press Enter.

53
00:02:13.301 --> 00:02:14.984
Not one bead moved.

54
00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:17.840
The porter just reads a different row now.

55
00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:21.080
And a label with no row at all gets a blank icon,

56
00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.296
and a question: which app should open this file?

57
00:02:25.745 --> 00:02:28.055
Windows and Mac still hide most extensions

58
00:02:28.055 --> 00:02:31.167
by default, and neither has announced a change.

59
00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:33.280
The tag is folded at the last dot,

60
00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:35.416
so you only see the front.

61
00:02:35.416 --> 00:02:40.165
A file really named hello.py.txt

62
00:02:40.165 --> 00:02:42.370
shows up as hello.py.

63
00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.019
It's still a text file.

64
00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:46.237
You can't fix a label you can't see.

65
00:02:46.237 --> 00:02:49.627
Showing extensions is one setting on each system.

66
00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:52.901
On recent Windows, it sits on a settings page

67
00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:55.049
Windows now calls Advanced.

68
00:02:55.049 --> 00:02:57.731
A Mac can also hide the extension per file,

69
00:02:57.731 --> 00:03:00.831
so two files in one folder can disagree.

70
00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:02.758
People who build things nearly always

71
00:03:02.758 --> 00:03:04.780
switch extensions on.

72
00:03:05.230 --> 00:03:07.645
Renaming rewrites the tag.

73
00:03:07.645 --> 00:03:10.257
The beads stay put.

74
00:03:10.257 --> 00:03:12.405
Microsoft says it plainly:

75
00:03:12.405 --> 00:03:15.040
renaming won't convert the file.

76
00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:17.014
That's why Aaron's fix worked.

77
00:03:17.014 --> 00:03:20.532
His beads were already Python text; the .txt

78
00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:23.214
tag was just sending them to the wrong door.

79
00:03:23.214 --> 00:03:29.530
But rename photo.png to .jpg and nothing converts.

80
00:03:29.530 --> 00:03:31.863
It may still open, because photo apps

81
00:03:31.863 --> 00:03:35.114
read the beads, but it's still a PNG.

82
00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:37.703
Real conversion happens inside an app,

83
00:03:37.703 --> 00:03:39.862
with Save As or Export.

84
00:03:39.862 --> 00:03:42.126
The warning you get when you change an extension

85
00:03:42.126 --> 00:03:45.226
is the porter saying: you're moving rows.

86
00:03:45.226 --> 00:03:48.361
And the classic trap: with extensions hidden,

87
00:03:48.361 --> 00:03:51.960
or Notepad saving as a text document, typing

88
00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:58.485
hello.py as the name can end up as hello.py.txt.

89
00:03:58.485 --> 00:04:00.797
It looks right. It isn't.

90
00:04:01.247 --> 00:04:03.557
Open with is you telling the porter

91
00:04:03.557 --> 00:04:06.263
which door, for this one file.

92
00:04:06.263 --> 00:04:08.317
There are two kinds of answer.

93
00:04:08.317 --> 00:04:10.384
A one-time choice opens the file

94
00:04:10.384 --> 00:04:13.147
in that program and leaves the ledger alone.

95
00:04:13.147 --> 00:04:15.074
An always choice picks up the pen

96
00:04:15.074 --> 00:04:17.489
and rewrites the row, so every file

97
00:04:17.489 --> 00:04:19.997
with that label goes there from now on.

98
00:04:19.997 --> 00:04:22.969
On a Mac, picking a different app in a file's

99
00:04:22.969 --> 00:04:25.999
info window changes only that one file,

100
00:04:25.999 --> 00:04:27.740
unless you press Change All.

101
00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.376
So a Mac can remember a choice per file.

102
00:04:30.376 --> 00:04:32.711
Windows remembers per label.

103
00:04:33.161 --> 00:04:35.425
Default apps, in Windows Settings,

104
00:04:35.425 --> 00:04:38.467
is the ledger itself, opened like a book.

105
00:04:38.467 --> 00:04:42.147
A default app is just the program a row points to.

106
00:04:42.147 --> 00:04:45.514
You can look it up by label, one row at a time.

107
00:04:45.514 --> 00:04:47.929
Or look it up by app: pick a browser,

108
00:04:47.929 --> 00:04:49.763
and see every label it can open,

109
00:04:49.763 --> 00:04:51.981
and who holds each row now.

110
00:04:51.981 --> 00:04:54.883
Here's proof the ledger works label by label.

111
00:04:54.883 --> 00:04:57.530
When Windows eleven launched in twenty twenty-one,

112
00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.260
switching your browser meant changing

113
00:04:59.260 --> 00:05:01.396
each label one by one.

114
00:05:01.396 --> 00:05:03.277
A one-click Set default button

115
00:05:03.277 --> 00:05:04.962
came back the next spring.

116
00:05:05.425 --> 00:05:07.155
In Europe, since last year,

117
00:05:07.155 --> 00:05:09.929
that button rewrites many more rows at once.

118
00:05:09.929 --> 00:05:12.381
Same ledger, a bigger pen.

119
00:05:12.832 --> 00:05:15.258
You're not the only one holding a pen.

120
00:05:15.258 --> 00:05:18.416
Installers write rows: install a code editor,

121
00:05:18.416 --> 00:05:21.760
or Python, and it can claim .py.

122
00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:23.583
Updates write rows too.

123
00:05:23.583 --> 00:05:25.835
A twenty twenty-three update taught Windows

124
00:05:25.835 --> 00:05:30.421
to open seven Z and RAR archives itself.

125
00:05:30.421 --> 00:05:32.685
Since twenty twenty-three, Microsoft's rule

126
00:05:32.685 --> 00:05:35.402
is that apps should send you to Settings to ask,

127
00:05:35.402 --> 00:05:37.654
not quietly take the row.

128
00:05:37.654 --> 00:05:39.094
Since twenty twenty-four,

129
00:05:39.094 --> 00:05:41.730
Windows guards some rows, like web links

130
00:05:41.730 --> 00:05:44.702
and PD Fs, so only you can change them.

131
00:05:44.702 --> 00:05:45.816
And since last year,

132
00:05:45.816 --> 00:05:48.579
it records your choice in a new, locked form.

133
00:05:48.579 --> 00:05:50.100
One side effect: sometimes,

134
00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.341
after an upgrade, a new default doesn't stick

135
00:05:52.341 --> 00:05:54.419
until you reset your defaults.

136
00:05:54.419 --> 00:05:57.579
And people still report apps grabbing file types.

137
00:05:58.028 --> 00:05:59.700
Here's where the picture breaks:

138
00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:01.732
there isn't one ledger.

139
00:06:01.732 --> 00:06:04.402
Windows keeps its table in the registry.

140
00:06:04.402 --> 00:06:06.759
A Mac maps the label to a type,

141
00:06:06.759 --> 00:06:08.372
then the type to an app,

142
00:06:08.372 --> 00:06:10.764
and keeps per-file choices.

143
00:06:10.764 --> 00:06:13.678
Linux desktops also peek at the contents,

144
00:06:13.678 --> 00:06:16.581
and keep your choices in a plain text file.

145
00:06:16.581 --> 00:06:19.158
And the porter only works the front door:

146
00:06:19.158 --> 00:06:20.946
double-click, Open with,

147
00:06:20.946 --> 00:06:23.477
the system's own open command.

148
00:06:23.477 --> 00:06:28.864
Name the program yourself, python notes.txt,

149
00:06:28.864 --> 00:06:31.081
and no ledger is consulted.

150
00:06:31.081 --> 00:06:34.564
So double-clicking script.py and typing

151
00:06:34.564 --> 00:06:38.349
python script.py can run different Pythons.

152
00:06:38.349 --> 00:06:40.660
The double-click runs whichever

153
00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:43.400
Python the ledger points at,

154
00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.778
not the one in your project's .venv.

155
00:06:46.778 --> 00:06:49.715
And on the web, the server's label decides,

156
00:06:49.715 --> 00:06:51.505
not the extension.

157
00:06:51.955 --> 00:06:54.579
Programs keep private ledgers too.

158
00:06:54.579 --> 00:06:57.783
VS Code reads the extension to pick its colours

159
00:06:57.783 --> 00:07:00.813
and checks, which it calls the language mode,

160
00:07:00.813 --> 00:07:02.230
and you can remap it.

161
00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:08.127
So hello.py.txt opens in Notepad,

162
00:07:08.127 --> 00:07:11.645
and gets no Python colours in VS Code either.

163
00:07:11.645 --> 00:07:13.897
Two ledgers, one label.

164
00:07:13.897 --> 00:07:17.334
With no label at all, it guesses from the content.

165
00:07:17.334 --> 00:07:18.681
Around AI,

166
00:07:18.681 --> 00:07:21.897
labels are promises about how to load a file.

167
00:07:21.897 --> 00:07:25.287
Older model files, like .ckpt,

168
00:07:25.287 --> 00:07:27.226
could run code when loaded;

169
00:07:27.226 --> 00:07:30.245
.safetensors was made so they can't.

170
00:07:30.245 --> 00:07:33.983
.gguf marks a local model.

171
00:07:33.983 --> 00:07:36.224
And MCP bundles are zips wearing

172
00:07:36.224 --> 00:07:39.056
their own label, like a Word file.

173
00:07:39.056 --> 00:07:42.911
Renaming one to .safetensors doesn't make it safe.

174
00:07:42.911 --> 00:07:45.014
It just won't load.

175
00:07:45.465 --> 00:07:47.590
Double-click a .py file,

176
00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:50.237
and what happens depends on the machine.

177
00:07:50.237 --> 00:07:51.258
Often it runs,

178
00:07:51.258 --> 00:07:53.847
and the window closes before you can read it.

179
00:07:53.847 --> 00:07:56.912
It ran; it didn't open for editing.

180
00:07:56.912 --> 00:07:58.956
A box asking which app to open

181
00:07:58.956 --> 00:08:01.452
it with means the label has no row.

182
00:08:01.452 --> 00:08:03.600
A file with no extension at all gets

183
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:06.328
a blank icon and the same question.

184
00:08:06.328 --> 00:08:09.346
Sometimes a default won't stick after an upgrade.

185
00:08:09.346 --> 00:08:10.449
That's a side effect

186
00:08:10.449 --> 00:08:12.412
of the locked record from earlier,

187
00:08:12.412 --> 00:08:15.442
and resetting your defaults usually fixes it.

188
00:08:15.442 --> 00:08:17.578
The icon changed after a rename,

189
00:08:17.578 --> 00:08:19.366
but the file won't open?

190
00:08:19.366 --> 00:08:21.595
The new door can't read those beads.

191
00:08:21.595 --> 00:08:25.578
And invoice.pdf.exe is the folded tag's

192
00:08:25.578 --> 00:08:28.526
other face: a program in a costume.

193
00:08:28.526 --> 00:08:31.140
The Trust district covers that one.

194
00:08:31.590 --> 00:08:33.494
Five years ago, you created

195
00:08:33.494 --> 00:08:35.503
and named every file yourself,

196
00:08:35.503 --> 00:08:38.289
and AI was autocomplete inside it.

197
00:08:38.289 --> 00:08:40.251
Since twenty twenty-three,

198
00:08:40.251 --> 00:08:42.933
and everywhere by now, assistants like Claude

199
00:08:42.933 --> 00:08:46.729
and ChatGPT hand you real Word, Excel,

200
00:08:46.729 --> 00:08:50.177
PowerPoint and PDF files, and coding agents

201
00:08:50.177 --> 00:08:53.335
write project files with labels they choose.

202
00:08:53.335 --> 00:08:55.553
When an agent shows you a file,

203
00:08:55.553 --> 00:08:58.304
it usually asks the same porter.

204
00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:00.928
When it runs code, it names the program

205
00:09:00.928 --> 00:09:02.858
and walks past the ledger.

206
00:09:03.808 --> 00:09:05.770
Windows has started adding AI

207
00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:08.022
actions to the right-click menu,

208
00:09:08.022 --> 00:09:10.693
and which ones you get depends on the label:

209
00:09:10.693 --> 00:09:14.547
image tools only for JPG and PNG.

210
00:09:14.547 --> 00:09:17.101
Windows is also previewing a way for agents

211
00:09:17.101 --> 00:09:20.317
to reach files, only in folders you allow;

212
00:09:20.317 --> 00:09:22.918
it still reports the extension separately.

213
00:09:22.918 --> 00:09:25.403
Mac OS twenty-seven now suggests

214
00:09:25.403 --> 00:09:27.237
file names from what's inside,

215
00:09:27.237 --> 00:09:29.257
but the label still picks the app.

216
00:09:29.257 --> 00:09:31.893
The direction looks like more rows only

217
00:09:31.893 --> 00:09:34.377
you can change, and more doors for agents

218
00:09:34.377 --> 00:09:36.353
that need your permission.

219
00:09:36.802 --> 00:09:38.543
Safe to ignore for now:

220
00:09:38.543 --> 00:09:40.924
the registry, prog I Ds,

221
00:09:40.924 --> 00:09:43.977
Mac type identifiers, Linux mime files,

222
00:09:43.977 --> 00:09:46.705
and every menu path. Keep this.

223
00:09:46.705 --> 00:09:49.898
One: the extension is a label in the name.

224
00:09:49.898 --> 00:09:52.359
Two: your computer keeps a ledger

225
00:09:52.359 --> 00:09:54.495
from label to program.

226
00:09:54.495 --> 00:09:57.897
Three: renaming changes the label;

227
00:09:57.897 --> 00:09:59.279
Open with's Always

228
00:09:59.279 --> 00:10:01.868
and default apps change the ledger.

229
00:10:01.868 --> 00:10:04.027
Next on the map: folders,

230
00:10:04.027 --> 00:10:06.502
paths and the working directory.
